formal:
Let \(\sigma\) be the arithmetic sentence asserting that Boolean satisfiability has a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm, with the finite encodings specified below. Is \(\sigma\) independent of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice (ZFC)? Precisely, is the following metamathematical proposition true?
\[\mathrm{ZFC}
vdash\sigma\quad	ext{and}\quad\mathrm{ZFC}
vdash
eg\sigma.\]

Is the following classification true? For every integer \(k\ge1\), both equivalences hold:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\forall\varphi:\mathbb R_{\ge0}^{k}\to\{0,1\},&
\varphi\text{ is robustly decidable}
&\iff \varphi\text{ is a multi-threshold predicate};\\
&\forall f:\mathbb R_{\ge0}^{k}\to\mathbb R_{\ge0},&
f\text{ is robustly computable}
&\iff f\text{ is threshold-piecewise rational floor-affine}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The terms on both sides are defined below. The question is the conjunction of the predicate and numerical-output classification clauses, in the continuous mass-action chemical reaction model with fixed initial context. Robustness quantifies over every assignment of positive rate constants that remain fixed throughout a trajectory.

definitions:
Fix an infinite set D of data values. An input schema is a finite set of relation names with specified finite arities. A database instance I is a finite set of facts \(R(a_{1},\ldots ,a_{r})\), with \(a_{i}\in D\). Its active domain \(adom(I)\) is the set of values occurring in those facts. J is an induced extension of I when \(adom(I)\subseteq adom(J)\) and, for every input relation R of arity r, Rᴵ=Rᴶ∩\(adom(I)\)ʳ. Thus all relations on old tuples are unchanged; every new fact of positive arity contains at least one new value. Nullary facts must remain unchanged. A query q of arity k maps I to a finite set of k-tuples, and is generic if permuting D commutes with q. For \(k=0\), the empty tuple represents true. Membership in E means \(q(I)\subseteq q(J)\) for every such pair I,J. A program has finitely many rules H(ȳ)←\(A_{1},\ldots ,A_{u},
eg B_{1},\ldots ,
eg B_{v},z_{1}
e w_{1},\ldots ,z_{s}
e w_{s}\). Atoms use relation names and variables, with no constants, function symbols or value invention. Every variable in the head, a negative atom or a disequality must also occur in a positive body atom. Input relations are extensional and never defined by a rule; derived relations are intensional. A stratification assigns levels to intensional relations so that positive dependencies have no higher level than the head and negative dependencies have strictly lower level. Evaluate levels in order, starting new derived relations empty and repeatedly adding every head whose instantiated body holds until no further fact is added; lower levels and input facts stay fixed. The distinguished output relation defines q. A semi-positive program permits negative atoms only on input relations. Equality can be represented by repeated variables, and disequality is actual inequality of data values. Programs may introduce finitely many auxiliary intensional relations of arbitrary finite arity. The requested equivalence is equality of outputs on every finite input instance, with no built-in order or additional input relations.
